#doc (Manual) "The Orlik–Solomon theorem from the axioms of cohomology" =>
%%%
shortTitle := "Orlik–Solomon from axioms"
authors := ["Adam Topaz", "Claude"]
%%%

This book explains a formalization, in Lean and Mathlib, of the Orlik–Solomon structure theorem for the cohomology of complements of hyperplane arrangements, in affine space and in projective space.
It is written for a working mathematician who wants to know what exactly is being proved, from what, and how the proof is organized.
No knowledge of Lean is needed.

{statusSummary}

*The theorem.*
Fix a field $`k` and a finite set $`\mathcal{A}` of distinct hyperplanes defined over $`k` in affine space $`\mathbb{A}^n_k`, and let $`U_{\mathcal{A}}` be the complement of their union.
The classical theorem of Orlik and Solomon presents the cohomology of the complement of a complex arrangement by generators and relations that can be read off from the incidences of the hyperplanes.
Here the cohomology theory is not fixed.
It is any theory with bigraded groups $`H^{p,w}`, where $`p` is the cohomological degree and $`w` is the Tate twist, that satisfies the axioms listed below.
Write
$$`B=\bigoplus_{w\in\mathbb{Z}}H^{0,w}(k),\qquad C(X)=\bigoplus_{(p,w)\in\mathbb{Z}^2}H^{p,w}(X)`
for the cohomology of the point and the total cohomology of $`X`, with coefficient ring $`R=B_0`.

The _Orlik–Solomon algebra_ $`\operatorname{OS}_B(\mathcal{A})` is the quotient of the exterior algebra over $`B` on generators $`e_H`, one for each hyperplane, by a two-sided ideal.
For an ordered list $`I=(H_1,\ldots,H_s)` of distinct hyperplanes, put $`e_I=e_{H_1}\cdots e_{H_s}` and
$$`\partial e_I=\sum_{j=1}^s(-1)^{j-1}e_{H_1}\cdots\widehat{e_{H_j}}\cdots e_{H_s}.`
The ideal is generated by $`e_I` when $`\bigcap I` is empty, and by $`\partial e_I` when $`\bigcap I` is nonempty of codimension less than $`s`.

Choose an equation $`f_H` for each hyperplane.
It is invertible on $`U_{\mathcal{A}}`, so it has a logarithmic class $`[f_H]` of bidegree $`(1,1)`, constructed from the axioms.
The aim is a canonical isomorphism of bigraded $`B`-algebras, where $`e_H` has bidegree $`(1,1)` and $`B_w` has bidegree $`(0,w)`:
$$`\Phi_{\mathcal{A}}:\operatorname{OS}_B(\mathcal{A})\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }C(U_{\mathcal{A}}),\qquad e_H\longmapsto[f_H].`
It does not depend on the choice of equations.
Taking homogeneous components gives
$$`H^{p,w}(U_{\mathcal{A}})\simeq\operatorname{OS}_R^p(\mathcal{A})\otimes_R B_{w-p}\quad(0\le p\le n),`
with $`H^{p,w}(U_{\mathcal{A}})=0` for other $`p`, and the diagonal $`\bigoplus_q H^{q,q}(U_{\mathcal{A}})` is canonically $`\operatorname{OS}_R(\mathcal{A})`.
The base field is arbitrary, the coefficients need not form a field, and no generator of any Tate line is ever chosen.
This is {bp "thm:affine"}[] together with {bp "cor:ordinary"}[].

For a nonempty arrangement $`\mathcal{P}` of hyperplanes in $`\mathbb{P}^n_k`, with central cone $`c\mathcal{P}`, the corresponding statement uses the subalgebra $`\overline{\operatorname{OS}}_B(\mathcal{P})` of $`\operatorname{OS}_B(c\mathcal{P})` generated by differences of generators.
The aim there is a canonical isomorphism
$$`\overline{\operatorname{OS}}_B(\mathcal{P})\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }C(U_{\mathcal{P}}),\qquad e_i-e_j\longmapsto[\ell_i/\ell_j],`
independent of the equations and of any hyperplane chosen at infinity.
This is {bp "thm:projective"}[].

*What is conditional.*
The theorem is conditional on a structure supplying the cohomology axioms, and this is the most important thing to understand about it.
In Lean, the cohomology theory is an arbitrary value of a structure whose fields are the axioms: products, a dimension axiom at the point, affine-line invariance, and compatible localization and divisor purity.
They are spelled out in {bp "def:cohomology"}[].
The results are quantified over every such structure, and no global Lean axiom is added.

To obtain an unconditional theorem about a named theory, such as Betti, $`\ell`-adic, algebraic de Rham or rigid cohomology, one must construct an instance of this structure for that theory, which includes supplying its localization and purity theorems.
This project constructs no such instance, and does not claim that one is available in Lean's libraries.
The dimension axiom also marks a real boundary: it says that the point has cohomology only in cohomological degree zero, and arithmetic cohomology over a nonclosed field and many generalized theories fail it.
They are outside this theorem.
The blueprint's separate additive treatment of those theories is {ref "generalized"}[deferred].
In the projective case, the empty arrangement is excluded, because the cohomology of $`\mathbb{P}^n` needs a projective bundle formula that the axioms do not provide.

The axioms are deliberately much weaker than the conclusion.
No axiom asserts that logarithmic classes exist or generate, that the arrangement relations hold, that residues are surjective, that Gysin maps vanish, that a deletion sequence is short exact, that anything is free, or the structure theorem itself.
All of these are outputs of the proof.

*How this book is tied to the code.*
Every result appears in a box that shows its label in the [blueprint](https://github.com/adamtopaz/OrlikSolomon/blob/main/blueprint/arrangement_cohomology_lean_blueprint.tex) and a status:
 * *Planned* means that the result is in scope and no Lean declaration is attached to it yet. The text describes what is intended, and nothing has been checked.
 * *Deferred* means that the result is in the blueprint but outside the current scope of the project.
 * *Partly formalized* means that some of the result has been checked, and the box says what is missing.
 * *Formalized* means that the box names Lean declarations, and shows their statements with links to their source.

A box cannot claim the last two statuses by assertion.
They follow from naming declarations, and the build of this book fails if a named declaration does not exist, or if it depends on `sorryAx` or on any axiom other than Lean's standard `propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound`.
A second check fails the build if the blueprint has a node that the book does not present.
The {ref "status"}[status page] is generated from the same data.
Prose outside the boxes is not checked by Lean, so when the text and a box disagree about what has been done, the box is right.

